WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.340
Dry, ferment and jar food products that sell.

2
00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:10.850
This is the video for Module 4 of Advanced Cooking and Nutrition: food preservation and packaged products.

3
00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:15.410
Preserving food lets you buy produce when it is cheap.

4
00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:20.180
It cuts waste when the market is full of tomatoes or mango.

5
00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:28.410
And it creates products like dried tomato, ogi, pickles, yaji and pepper sauce that sell between events.

6
00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:36.680
The secret is consistency. The hundredth jar must taste exactly like the first, and every one must be safe.

7
00:00:37.930 --> 00:00:50.580
Microbes need water to grow, so removing water makes food keep for months. Step one. Sort, wash and slice evenly, about 6 to 8 mm thick, so pieces dry at the same speed.

8
00:00:51.830 --> 00:01:02.220
Step two. Blanch leafy vegetables in boiling water for about 1 to 2 minutes, then cool them quickly. Dip mango in diluted lemon juice to limit browning.

9
00:01:03.470 --> 00:01:11.420
Step three. Dry in single layers on clean mesh trays in a covered solar dryer, not on the ground or by the roadside.

10
00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:20.500
Step four. Test it. Vegetables and ginger should snap. Mango should be leathery with no moisture when you tear and squeeze it.

11
00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:29.280
Step five. Cool before packing. Warm food packed in a bag sweats and goes mouldy. Pack airtight, label and date it.

12
00:01:30.530 --> 00:01:38.060
Throw away any batch with visible mould or a musty smell. Some moulds produce toxins you cannot see or remove.

13
00:01:39.310 --> 00:01:51.070
In fermentation, friendly bacteria turn sugars into acid, which keeps harmful bacteria away. For ogi, steep clean maize for 1 to 3 days, changing the water daily.

14
00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:59.150
Mill it, sieve it with clean water, then let the starch settle and sour. Keep it cool and use within a few days.

15
00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:14.450
For brined vegetables, weigh the salt. For example, Ruth in Jos used 2,000 g of cabbage and carrots, so 2,000 times 0.02 gives 40 g of salt. Spoons vary too much.

16
00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:21.930
Pack tightly and keep the vegetables under the brine. When it tastes pleasantly sour, move it to the fridge.

17
00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:32.480
Vinegar pickles need vinegar labelled 5% acidity. Never dilute it more than the tested recipe says, because the acid keeps it safe.

18
00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:42.870
Fresh ogi and good ferments have a clean, pleasantly sour smell. A thin white film on brine is often a harmless yeast and can be skimmed.

19
00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:54.760
But fuzzy mould of any colour, pink, grey or black patches, a slimy texture or a rotten smell mean the whole batch goes. Never taste a ferment that smells bad.

20
00:02:56.010 --> 00:03:13.270
Write every formula as a percentage of the total weight, and any batch is just multiplication. Hadiza's suya spice is 55% kuli kuli powder, 20% dried chilli, 10% ginger, and 5% each of garlic, onion and salt.

21
00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:24.650
For 5,000 g she needs 2,750 g of kuli kuli and 1,000 g of chilli. She checks the total adds back to 5,000 g.

22
00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:33.580
Ingredients cost about 27,500 naira, and packs and labels about 7,500 naira.

23
00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:41.930
That makes 50 packs of 100 g, at about 700 naira each before her time, gas and profit.

24
00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:51.910
The total is about 35,000 naira. Because her main ingredient is groundnut, her label must state the groundnut allergen clearly.

25
00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:59.840
Wash jars in hot soapy water, rinse, then keep them in boiling water for 10 minutes until you fill them.

26
00:04:01.090 --> 00:04:02.580
Cook the sauce thoroughly.

27
00:04:03.830 --> 00:04:07.900
Fill it while very hot, leaving about 1 cm of space at the top.

28
00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:12.160
Wipe the rim with a clean cloth and close it at once.

29
00:04:13.410 --> 00:04:23.350
Sauces with garlic, onion or pepper under oil can allow botulism to grow if not cooked and acidified properly. It is rare but can be fatal.

30
00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:31.490
Keep opened jars in the fridge, and never claim months of shelf life at room temperature unless a food laboratory has tested it.

31
00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:40.230
To remember. Dry in a covered dryer and cool before packing. Throw away any mouldy batch. Weigh your salt for ferments.

32
00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:49.490
Write formulas in percentages and weigh every ingredient. Use sterilised jars and label sauces to refrigerate after opening.

33
00:04:50.740 --> 00:05:02.240
Your practice: dry and weigh one product and work out your yield, make one ferment and one pickle with a daily log, and formulate, scale and cost your own spice blend or sauce.

34
00:05:02.590 --> 00:05:04.530
Continue with the lessons below.
